import BlueprintPage from '../components/BlueprintPage.astro'; const sections=[
{title:'Is remote care suitable?',paragraphs:['Some follow-up and counselling conversations may be possible remotely. Eligibility depends on identity, consent, privacy, connection, availability, clinical need, and safety. An online appointment is not automatically appropriate for every concern. The clinician may recommend an in-person visit, examination, assessment tool, local referral or a different service if remote care cannot answer the question safely.','Before requesting, consider whether you can speak privately and whether your internet connection is reliable. If you are sharing a device or living space, ask how to arrange a confidential conversation. For a child, contact Aasha and ask whether a remote first conversation is appropriate for the child and family.']},
{title:'Questions to ask before booking online',paragraphs:['Ask which clinician will meet you, what platform will be used, how appointment details are shared, what the fee is, and what happens if the call disconnects. Confirm how identity, location and consent are checked and whether a phone number will be used as a backup. Ask how records and follow-up work, and whether prescriptions or referrals can be discussed remotely for your situation.','An online appointment should take place from a setting where you can speak openly without being overheard. Use a private connection where possible, charge the device and keep the clinic contact information available. Do not forward appointment links or record a session unless the clinician and all participants have agreed to that arrangement. Read the clinic <a href="/privacy">privacy notice</a> and <a href="/teleconsultation">teleconsultation information</a>.']},
{title:'Therapy, psychiatry and continuity of care',paragraphs:['Online therapy focuses on psychological support with a qualified provider within their scope. Telepsychiatry involves a medical consultation and may include discussion of treatment options. The roles are different, and either may be unsuitable for a particular person or question. See <a href="/therapy">therapy at Mind Grace</a>, <a href="/psychiatry">psychiatry consultations</a> and the <a href="/psychiatrist-vs-psychologist">comparison of professional roles</a>.','If you already receive care elsewhere, ask whether the clinician can coordinate with your existing provider and what consent is required. Ask how follow-up is arranged if you travel or relocate, and what local support should be available if symptoms worsen between appointments. Keep your current clinician informed about changes to treatment; do not alter prescribed medicines based only on an online appointment article.']},
{title:'Limits and emergencies',paragraphs:['Remote care may require in-person review or referral. Do not use online channels for immediate danger; call 112. A routine video appointment cannot replace emergency evaluation, and a messaging inbox may not be monitored continuously. If someone cannot stay safe, seek urgent local help rather than waiting for an appointment.','For routine scheduling at Mind Grace, review <a href="/fees">fees</a> and use <a href="/book">the appointment request page</a>. The clinic confirms whether a remote visit is available and suitable. A submitted request does not confirm an appointment time.']}
];
---
